145.05 How to Report (FSM)

SR 25-23 Dated 06/25 Previous Policy

_____________________________________________________________

Individuals may report changes in any of the following ways:

• online through the individual ’ s NH EASY account;

• by mail;

• by telephone;

• in person; or

• through someone acting on the individual ’ s behalf.

References : He-W 752.01; RSA 161:4-a,IV;

231 CATEGORICALLY AND EXPANDED CATEGORICALLY ELIGIBLE HOUSEHOLDS (FSM)

SR 26-12 Dated 03/26 Previous Policy

____________________________________________________________

Certain SNAP households may be considered Categorically Eligible or Expanded Categorically Eligible based on meeting certain criteria. These households are subject to fewer eligibility requirements than regular SNAP households.

See Section 231.01 Categorical Eligibility Criteria and Section 231.05 Expanded Categorical Eligibility Criteria for more information.

A household cannot be considered Categorically Eligible or Expanded Categorically Eligible when:

· The household includes a member who has been suspended from receiving SSI due to noncompliance with drug and/or alcohol abuse treatment requirements (categorical eligibility does not apply until the suspension ends and the member begins receiving SSI again);

· The household includes someone disqualified due to an intentional program violation;

· The household includes someone disqualified because of noncompliance with SNAP work requirements due to Voluntary Quit;

· The household includes a fleeing felon, certain convicted felons, or probation or parole violator;

· A household closed for substantial lottery or gambling winnings, until the household has certified under regular SNAP rules.

References : RSA 161:4-a,IV; RSA 167:4,I(a); 7 CFR 273.2(j)(2) & (j)(4); 7 CFR 273.9(a); 7 CFR 273.11(r)

245.03 Exceptions from SNAP ABAWD Work Requirements (FSM)

SR 26-24 Dated 09/26 Previous Policy

____________________________________________________________

If an individual does not meet an exemption from SNAP general work requirements, they are subject to SNAP ABAWD work requirements. Individuals will be screened for all exceptions at initial application, recertification, and if there is a change in status from exempt to mandatory. The ABAWD work requirements do not apply if the individual meets one of the below exceptions:

· A member of a SNAP household that includes a child under age 14 who lives in the home, or who is temporarily absent from the home and intends to return, regardless of the ABAWD individual’s relationship to or responsibility for the minor child, and regardless of the minor child’s eligibility for SNAP benefits.

o The child under age 14 must be considered a member of the ABAWD individual’s SNAP household or would be considered a member of the ABAWD individual’s household if not excluded or disqualified from being a member in that household.

o If the child is excluded from receiving SNAP in one household because the parents have 50/50 custody and the child is already receiving SNAP in the other parent’s household, both parents are exempt from ABAWD work requirements.

· Exempt from the SNAP general work requirements;

· Aged 65 or older;

· Under the age of 18;

· Pregnant;

· Residing in certain areas with a high unemployment rate;

· An Indian, Urban Indian, California Indian, or other Indigenous individual that is eligible for Indian Health Services; or

· Medically certified as physically or mentally unfit for employment. An individual is medically certified as physically or mentally unfit for employment if they have limitations that prevent them from obtaining or retaining employment, whether temporary or permanent. This includes, but is not limited to:

o physical illness;

o injury;

o disability;

o mental health issues that negatively impacts ability to work;

o facing significant barriers; or

o are obviously mentally or physically unfit for employment. If unfitness is not obvious (questionable), a statement is needed from a medical professional that verifies the individual is physically or mentally unfit for employment.

An individual who meets a SNAP general work requirement exemption or SNAP ABAWD work requirement exception at any time during the month is considered exempt for the entire month. The exception that will be in effect the longest will be applied. SNAP ABAWD exceptions are self-attested unless questionable.

An individual who is exempt from ABAWD work requirements is not automatically eligible for SNAP benefits. The individual must also be eligible for SNAP benefits in all other respects.

Loss of Exemption Status

If an individual’s status changes from exempt to mandatory the Consolidated Work Notice will be sent to the household. Reevaluate the individual’s eligibility and take appropriate case action.

Exemption After Loss of Eligibility for Failure to Meet ABAWD Work Requirements

An individual who loses eligibility for SNAP benefits due to failure to meet ABAWD work requirements, but who later becomes exempt from those work requirements, is no longer subject to SNAP ABAWD work requirements until the exemption no longer exists.

References : 7 CFR 273.7(b); 7 CFR 273.7(c)(1)(ii); 7 CFR 273.7(c)(1)(iii); 7 CFR 273.24(c); 7 CFR 273.24(k); 7 USC 2015(o)(3)

249.03 Who Is Not Eligible For Extended Food Stamps (EFS) (FSM)

SR 23-28 Dated 08/23 Previous Policy

____________________________________________________________

Households are not eligible for EFS if FANF financial assistance closed in part or solely due to any of the following:

• a sanction or IPV;

• being a fleeing felon or a probation or parole violator;

• a transfer of assets or property to qualify for assistance;

• receipt of a lump sum;

• failure to cooperate with quality control or child support;

• failure to provide verification required to determine eligibility;

• failure to complete a recertification or attend a special review appointment;

• not residing in New Hampshire;

• a client requests the financial assistance case to be closed, unless the request is due to saving FANF months on the lifetime limit; or

• a casehead has left the household.

Households in which all members are ineligible for SNAP benefits are also ineligible for EFS.

Individuals ineligible for or excluded from SNAP at the time that EFS is initially determined will remain ineligible for the duration of the EFS period.

References : He-W 734.02; RSA 161:4-a,IV; 7 CFR 273.11; 7 CFR 273.26; 7 USC 2020(s)(5)

149 MASS CHANGES (FSM)

SR 13-19 Dated 09/13 Previous Policy

_____________________________________________________________

The state or federal government initiates mass changes that can affect all individuals or large numbers of individuals. Mass changes include the following:

• income eligibility standards;

• cost-of-living adjustments for Social Security, SSI, VA, and other federal benefits;

• other eligibility criteria based on legislation or regulations;

• utility standards;

• shelter deductions;

• dependent care deductions;

• standard deductions; or

• food stamp allotment amounts.

When mass changes occur:

• eligibility or benefits are adjusted automatically; and

• a Notice of Decision (NOD) is generated to notify individuals of the results of any changes.

If an individual requests an administrative appeal as a result of a decrease or termination in benefits, reinstate benefits to the pre-change level only if the appeal is requested within 15 days from the date on the notice of the action being appealed and the issue contested is that:

• eligibility or the amount of benefits was improperly computed; or

• a federal law or regulation was misapplied or misinterpreted.

References : RSA 161:4-a,IV , 7 CFR 273.12(e)

301.07 How to Verify (FSM)

SR 11-07 Dated 02/11 Previous Policy

__________________________________________________________________________

Verify identity using any document that reasonably establishes the individual’s identity. Identity can be verified through documentary evidence or through a collateral contact.

Examples of acceptable documentary proof for identification include, but are not limited to , the following:

• Driver’s License;

• Work or school identification card;

• Health benefits identification card;

• Identification from another assistance or social services program;

• A voter registration card;

• Wage stubs; or

• A birth certificate.

A picture ID is not required to establish the identity of the Food Stamp head of household. If an applicant is applying for more than one program, and the other program does require picture identification, that identification may also be used for verification of identity for Food Stamp purposes.

References : He-W 701.03; 7 CFR 273.2(f)(1)(vii)

305.13 Qualified Aliens Eligible Without Restriction (FSM)

SR 26-06 Dated 03/26 Previous Policy

__________________________________________________________________________

The following categories of qualified aliens are eligible for Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) benefits without restriction (eligible immediately, there is no 5-year waiting period) if all other SNAP financial and non-financial eligibility criteria are met:

· Cuban/Haitian Entrants;

· Non-citizen U.S. nationals;

· COFA Migrants- individuals who lawfully reside in the United States who have citizenship from the Federated States of Micronesia, the Republic of the Marshall Islands, or the Republic of Palau in accordance with the Compact of Free Association (COFA); and

· Lawful Permanent Residents (LPR) only if LPR also is meeting one or more of the following conditions;

o are under 18 years old;

o have 40 qualifying work quarters;

o are blind or disabled;

o were lawfully residing in the U.S. and 65 or older on August 22, 1996;

o have U.S. military connection;

o are admitted to the U.S. as an Amerasian immigrant;

o are a certain American Indian born abroad;

o are a certain Hmong or Highland Laotian tribal member;

o are a former Refugee;

o are a former Asylee;

o Individuals who are LPR whose deportation was withheld;

o Iraqi and Afghan Special Immigrants (SIV) Certain Afghan Nationals Granted Parole Between February 24, 2022-September 30, 2024 (Humanitarian Parolees) who have obtained LPR;

o Certain Ukrainian Nationals Granted Parole Between February 24, 2022-September 30, 2024 (Humanitarian Parolees)who have obtained LPR; or

o Victims of Severe trafficking who have obtained LPR.

References : 7 CFR 273.4(a)(2)-(6);, 8 USC 1611-1613, 1621, & 1641; 62 Federal Register (FR) 61348-61349 (Nov. 17, 1997)

LUMP SUM PAYMENT: NONRECURRING (RESOURCE) (FSM)

SR 26-12 Dated 03/26 Previous Policy

____________________________________________________________

Lump sum payments that are nonrecurring include:

· awards, windfalls, other prizes;

· lottery and gambling winnings;

· conversion of resources to cash;

· corrective payments;

· inheritances;

· insurance claims;

· retirement benefits;

· retroactive benefits or payments, including lump sum back child support payments;

· sale of property;

· settlement of personal injury or property claims;

· third-party lump sum medical payments. If payments for medical expenses were previously paid by Medicaid, send all details regarding lump sum medical payments to Third Party Liability (TPL), Medicaid Administration Bureau (MAB) at State Office. TPL will determine the dollar amount the individual must reimburse to the Department of Health and Human Services (DHHS);

· rebates or credits; and

· refunds of security deposits on rental property or utilities.

Treatment : Countable Resource in the month received , unless specifically excluded from consideration as a resource by other federal statute.

Note that households with substantial lottery and gambling winnings ( a cash prize which is equal to or greater than the SNAP resource limit for elderly or disabled households won in a single game, before taxes or other amounts are withheld) must be disqualified immediately. See FSM 247 , SUBSTANTIAL LOTTERY OR GAMBLING WINNINGS

Exception : VA payments to children (including adult children) of Vietnam veterans are excluded per 38 USC 1833(c).

Exception: Earned Income Tax Credit payments and income tax refunds are excluded as a resource for 12 months from receipt.

Exception: Lump sum payments for work performed over a period of more than 1 month are prorated and treated as earned income.

References : RSA 161:2, XIII; RSA 161:4-a, IV; RSA 167:4, I(a); RSA 167:7, IV; 7 CFR 273.8(c)(1); 7 CFR 273.9(c)(8); 38 USC 1833(c); 26 USC 6409; 7 CFR 273.11(r)

AMERICORPS (FSM)

SR 13-22 Dated 07/12 Previous Policy

_____________________________________________________________

Monthly living allowances, educational awards, child care allowances and health care benefits made under the National and Community Service Trust Act of 1993, P.L. 103-82 to AmeriCorps participants.

The AmeriCorps Network consists of 3 separate programs based on the participant ’ s age and type of service performed:

• AmeriCorps State and National (age 17 and older) Title II Programs, including:

- AmeriCorps Victims Assistance Program (AVAP) and AVAP Afterschool Program;

- City Year;

- Foster Grandparents;

- Green Corps;

- Goodwill Industries;

- Retired and Senior Volunteer Program (RSVP); and

- Student Conservation Association;

• AmeriCorps VISTA (Volunteers in Service to America, age 18 and older) Title I Programs, including:

- Campus Compact for New Hampshire;

- Families in Transition; and

- New Hampshire Catholic Charities

• AmeriCorps NCCC (National Civilian Community Corps, age 18 to 24) Title II Program.

Treatment:

• Child care payments, educational awards, and health care benefits are excluded income by federal mandate.

• Any payment under Title II of the Domestic Volunteer Service Act of 1973, P.L. 93-113, including the monthly living allowance from AmeriCorps State and National and AmeriCorps NCCC, is excluded income.

• For payments under Title I of the Domestic Volunteer Service Act of 1973, P.L. 93-113, to participants in AmeriCorps VISTA, see Volunteers in Service to America (VISTA) and Title I Programs.

BENEFITS (FSM)

SR 00-05 Dated 01/00 Previous Policy

_____________________________________________________________

Recurring income from sources such as:

• annuities (an amount payable yearly);

• disability or sickness payments;

• fraternal benefits;

• insurance payments;

• pensions;

• retirement plans;

• Disbursements from trusts or similar legal devices made to an individual, on behalf of an individual, or for the benefit of an individual, regardless of the type or source of trust;

• unemployment compensation; or

• worker ’ s compensation (insurance payments received for an on-the-job injury).

For benefit income that is not recurring, but is a lump sum settlement, see Lump Sum Income . See also SSA , SSI , and VA Benefits.

Treatment: Unearned Income

Exception:

Benefits specifically designated as payment for hospitalization or medical services are excluded income.

